# Hint the Browser in Advance

`will-change` tells the browser that an element is likely to be animated, so it can prepare optimisation layers:

```css
.slide-element {
  will-change: transform;
}
```
Use it sparingly and remove it after the animation ends (otherwise it keeps GPU memory allocated). Only apply right before the animation and remove when done.

# Respect User Accessibility Settings

Some users disable animations in their OS. Honor that with:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .animated {
    animation: none;
    transition: none;
  }
}
```
This is a WCAG requirement for motion sensitivity. Always provide a reduced‑motion version.

